   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "Consider the curve given by \\( xy + y^{2} = 6 \\), along which \\( y \\) is a differentiable function of \\( x \\) near the point \\( (1, 2) \\). Find the value of \\( \\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} \\) at the point \\( (1, 2) \\)."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": [
         "Rational",
         12,
         125
      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "Differentiate \\( xy + y^{2} = 6 \\) implicitly with respect to \\( x \\): \\( y + \\left(x + 2 y\\right)\\frac{dy}{dx} = 0 \\), so \\( \\frac{dy}{dx} = - \\frac{y}{x + 2 y} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Negate",
            [
               "Divide",
               "y",
               [
                  "Add",
                  "x",
                  [
                     "Multiply",
                     2,
                     "y"
                  ]
               ]
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "Differentiate \\( \\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{- y}{x + 2 y} \\) again with the quotient rule, treating \\( y \\) as a function of \\( x \\): \\( \\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} = \\frac{- \\frac{dy}{dx} \\left(x + 2 y\\right) + y \\left(2 \\frac{dy}{dx} + 1\\right)}{\\left(x + 2 y\\right)^{2}} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            -2,
            5
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "At \\( (1, 2) \\), \\( \\frac{dy}{dx} = - \\frac{2}{5} \\). Substitute this value and the coordinates into the second derivative.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            12,
            125
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "So \\( \\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} = \\frac{12}{125} \\) at \\( (1, 2) \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            12,
            125
         ]
      }
   ],
